# ============================ SYSADMIN 7 ============================
LESSONS_S7 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The system is read before it is felt", "11", "<p>This module covers monitoring: the Error Log, the scheduler and its job logs, the background job queue, the login and activity reads, storage and performance trends, the health page that assembles them, and the incident log that closes the loop.</p><p><b>The principle.</b> Every system state that matters can be discovered two ways: <b>read</b> from the system's own instruments while it is small, or <b>felt</b> by the business when it is large — the backup that quietly stopped, felt at the restore; the scheduler that died, felt when the accruals never posted; the storage that filled, felt when attachments start failing at month-end. Monitoring is choosing to read. Every instrument in this module already exists on the site; the module's work is the <b>habit</b> — the reads on the calendar, the numbers trended, the exceptions investigated.</p><p><b>The three reading rules.</b></p><p>1. <b>Trend, not point.</b> A single number means little; its direction means everything. Storage at 60% is a fact; storage that moved 40 → 50 → 60 in three months is a forecast. The health page (Chapter 7) exists to hold last month's numbers beside this month's.<br>2. <b>Exception, not census.</b> The reads are built to surface what changed — new error types, jobs that stopped, counts that jumped — not to re-read the unchanged. A five-minute daily read stays five minutes only if it is exception-shaped.<br>3. <b>Silence is a finding.</b> The statement run's queue spike that did not happen, the nightly backup that is not listed, the scheduler log with no entries since Tuesday — the absence of expected activity is the loudest signal in this module, and the one untrained readers miss, because nothing looks wrong.</p><p><b>The escalation posture.</b> Monitoring feeds two lanes: the site-custody findings the sysadmin fixes (a misconfigured notification looping, a permission error pattern), and the platform-custody findings raised to CloudERP.One <b>with evidence</b> — the five-item report of module 1, now armed with this module's instruments: the exact Error Log entries, the job log timestamps, the trend numbers. Monitoring is what turns \"the system is slow\" into a report the host can act on in one pass.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Error Log. Chapter 3: the scheduler and its logs. Chapter 4: background jobs and the queues. Chapter 5: logins, lockouts, and the Activity Log. Chapter 6: storage and performance trends. Chapter 7: the health page and the assembled reads. Chapter 8: the incident log and the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Error Log", "12", "<p>Open <b>Error Log</b> (awesome bar). Every unhandled failure inside the site writes an entry here — the site's own complaint channel, and the daily read's first stop.</p><p><b>Reading an entry.</b> Each record carries the timestamp, the method or context that failed, and the traceback — the technical text describing where and why. The sysadmin does not need to program to read it usefully; three fields carry the triage:</p><p>• <b>The first and last lines of the traceback</b> — the error's name and its immediate cause, often plain enough (\"PermissionError\", a missing value, a timeout).<br>• <b>The method/context</b> — which machinery failed: a report, a notification, a scheduled job, a document event.<br>• <b>The count and clustering</b> — one entry is an event; forty identical entries since 9am is a pattern with a start time, and the start time usually names the cause (what changed at 9am?).</p><p><b>The triage, as a procedure.</b></p><p>1. <b>New or known?</b> The daily read scans for error types not seen before. Known noise — the benign entries every site accumulates — is recognised and passed; a <b>new</b> type is investigated the day it appears, because day one it has a fresh trail and day thirty it has folklore.<br>2. <b>Site custody or platform custody?</b> The custody map's question, asked with better evidence now: a permission error on one user is module 2's territory; a repeating failure inside platform machinery is a raise — with the entry's full text copied whole into the five-item report (the error text is the single highest-value item a support report carries).<br>3. <b>User-facing or silent?</b> An error a user saw has a person to follow up with; an error nobody saw is this module's speciality — the failing notification, the dying import — found here or not at all.</p><p><b>The patterns worth memorising.</b></p><p>• <b>The 9am cluster</b> — errors starting when a change deployed: module 5's verify-live step missed something; the change lane's log names the suspect.<br>• <b>The nightly regular</b> — the same entry every night at the same minute: a scheduled job failing on schedule (Chapter 3 confirms which).<br>• <b>The permission drizzle</b> — scattered PermissionErrors from one role after a permission change: the change was too tight; module 2's diagnosis path.<br>• <b>The integration silence-with-errors</b> — the channel register's health check failing while its errors accumulate here: Chapter 6-of-module-6's procedures, with the entries as evidence.</p><p>Practical rule: the Error Log read is sixty seconds when clean — filter to the last day, scan the types, done. Its value compounds through the calendar: the sysadmin who reads daily recognises \"new\" on sight, which is the entire skill.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The scheduler and its logs", "12", "<p>Half the programme's automation runs on the <b>scheduler</b> — the machinery that fires time-based work: the nightly backups, the Auto Repeat entries (the Accounts track's monthly rent journal), the Notifications on their date rules, the Auto Email Reports, the statement run's schedule, the queue's dispatch. A dead or limping scheduler silently stops all of it at once, which makes this chapter's read the daily list's most consequential thirty seconds.</p><p><b>The instruments.</b></p><p>• <b>Scheduled Job Type</b> (awesome bar) — the catalogue: every scheduled job the site knows, with its frequency and whether it is stopped. The read here is the census — what should be running.<br>• <b>Scheduled Job Log</b> — the record: each job's executions with status and time. The read here is the pulse — what actually ran, and when it last did.</p><p><b>The daily pulse check.</b> Open the Scheduled Job Log, sorted newest first: entries from the last few minutes and hours confirm the scheduler lives. Then the two exception reads:</p><p>1. <b>Failures:</b> filter to failed status in the last day — each failure is an Error Log companion entry (Chapter 2's nightly-regular pattern lands here); repeated failure of one job is a raise or a fix depending on custody.<br>2. <b>Silence:</b> the last-execution ages. A job that should run hourly showing a last run of Tuesday is the finding — and if <b>all</b> jobs show Tuesday, the scheduler itself died Tuesday, and everything time-based has been off since: backups, reminders, queues. That discovery is a same-day, highest-severity raise — and the reason this read is daily, because every day undetected multiplies the recovery: the missed Auto Repeats to post manually, the queued documents to drain, the backup gap to acknowledge in the continuity log.</p><p><b>The business-side verification.</b> The logs prove the machinery ran; the tracks prove the <b>work</b> arrived — and both checks exist because each catches what the other misses:</p><p>• The Accounts close confirms the Auto Repeat entries posted (checklist line 2 — \"confirmed fired\").<br>• The queue's volume read confirms the statement run's spike (module 6).<br>• The backup presence check confirms the nightly set (module 4).</p><p>When a track-side check fails (\"the rent journal didn't post\"), this chapter is the diagnosis path: the Scheduled Job Log says whether the machinery ran; the Error Log says why not; the custody map says who fixes it.</p><p>Practical rule: after any platform update or incident recovery, the pulse check runs immediately even though it is \"not time\" — schedulers are the classic casualty of disturbed systems, and the thirty-second read is cheaper than discovering it at month-end.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Background jobs and the queues", "12", "<p>Beside the clock-driven scheduler, the site runs <b>background jobs</b> — work handed off to run behind the interface so the user is not held waiting: the large report, the bulk import's processing, the heavy document submissions, the email dispatch. This chapter is reading that machinery.</p><p><b>The instrument.</b> Open the <b>Background Jobs</b> page (awesome bar): the current queues with their jobs — queued, started, and failed — and each job's method and age. The healthy picture is a short, fast-moving list: jobs appear, run, vanish. The unhealthy pictures:</p><p>• <b>The growing queue</b> — jobs arriving faster than they finish: the site is under load or a worker has stalled. Users feel this as \"the system is slow\" and \"my import is stuck\"; the queue read converts the feeling into evidence (how many jobs, how old, which methods) for the raise.<br>• <b>The failed cluster</b> — failed jobs naming one method: the Error Log holds the why (Chapter 2's companion pattern); the fix follows custody.<br>• <b>The stuck head</b> — one long-running job blocking a queue: the age column names it; the raise carries it.</p><p><b>The user-facing translations.</b> Three standing situations where this page answers the front line:</p><p>1. <b>\"My import has been running forever.\"</b> The import's processing is a background job — find it: still running (patience, with an age estimate), failed (the Data Import document carries the error rows; module 3's procedure), or absent (it never queued — resubmit).<br>2. <b>\"The report never loads.\"</b> Heavy reports queue in the background; a queue backlog delays them all. The read distinguishes \"this report is heavy\" (one job, running) from \"the site is congested\" (many jobs, aging) — two different escalations.<br>3. <b>\"Emails are delayed.\"</b> The email dispatch rides this machinery too — the Email Queue (module 6) shows the documents; this page shows whether the machinery that drains it is moving. The pair together diagnose in one minute what either alone only suspects.</p><p><b>Where it sits in the calendar.</b> Not a standing daily line — the daily read's instruments (Error Log, Scheduled Job Log, Email Queue, backup presence) catch the failures that matter overnight. Background Jobs is the <b>on-demand instrument</b>: opened when slowness is reported, when an import stalls, when the queue is suspected — plus a weekly glance riding the performance read (Chapter 6) so its normal picture stays familiar. An instrument read only during incidents is read badly; the weekly glance is what keeps \"normal\" recognisable.</p><p>Practical rule: every \"the system is slow\" report gets this page opened <b>while the slowness is live</b> — the evidence evaporates with the queue. A screenshot of the live queue state is worth three paragraphs in the raise.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Logins, lockouts, and the Activity Log", "12", "<p>Who is using the system, from where, and who is failing to — the people-side reads, sitting between this module and module 8's security layer.</p><p><b>The instruments.</b></p><p>• <b>The User list's last-login read</b> (module 2 introduced it): Enabled users sorted by last login. The two ends carry the findings — never-logged-in accounts (granted but unused: a joiner who never started, or an account created for no one) and the ninety-day-quiet accounts the quarterly recertification disables.<br>• <b>The Activity Log</b> (awesome bar): the stream of logins and significant actions, filterable by user and time. The read is exception-shaped: <b>failed login clusters</b> (many failures on one account: a forgotten password if the owner confirms, a guessing attempt if not — the lockout setting from module 1's settings walk is the automatic answer, and the cluster is the read that proves it fired), <b>off-hours patterns</b> (logins at hours the job does not explain — a conversation, not an accusation: legitimate reasons abound, but the pattern is read, not ignored), and <b>the after-event reconstruction</b> (when module 8's audit questions arrive — who was in the system Tuesday evening — this log is the first answer).</p><p><b>The weekly read (module 1's calendar line, now fully specified).</b></p><p>1. Failed-login clusters in the last week: each cluster attributed (owner confirms the fumble) or escalated (module 8's security-event response for the unexplained).<br>2. Lockouts fired: read as the control working; repeated lockouts on one account get the owner conversation (training or trouble).<br>3. The user delta (module 2's read) — the same sitting, the same five minutes.</p><p><b>The session angle.</b> Module 1's settings walk set session expiry; this read verifies the policy is livable: complaints about constant re-login mean the expiry is too tight for the work pattern (a governed settings conversation, not a silent loosening), while sessions that never seem to end mean the setting drifted or a change reverted it — the annual walk's reconciliation catches the second; the complaints catch the first.</p><p><b>The privacy posture, stated once.</b> These reads exist to protect the system and its users — the honest account holder is <b>defended</b> by the log that shows their account guessed-at and locked, exactly as the honest cashier is defended by the till count. The reads are performed by the named sysadmin, on the calendar, for the written purposes; browsing colleagues' activity outside the reads is itself an access-review finding. The instrument that guards the system is governed like everything else that touches it.</p><p>Practical rule: the weekly people-read is five minutes because it is exception-shaped — clusters, lockouts, deltas. The month it takes twenty minutes is the month something is happening; that is the read working.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Storage and performance trends", "12", "<p>The slow-growing numbers: disk and speed. Neither fails suddenly — both drift, which makes them the purest case for the trend-not-point rule.</p><p><b>Storage.</b> The site's footprint is the database plus the files (module 4's two components, now read as sizes). The platform exposes the numbers through the host's panel or the support channel; the sysadmin records them <b>monthly on the health page</b> — database size, files size, and the deltas.</p><p>• <b>The reads:</b> steady growth proportional to trading is health; a <b>delta jump</b> names an event (a heavy import month, an attachment-happy new process, a runaway log) — identified while it is one month old, not archaeologised at 90% full.<br>• <b>The attachment economy:</b> files usually outgrow the database, because evidence habits (this programme's own doing) attach documents everywhere. The governance is sizing, not abstinence: module 1's settings walk set the attachment size limit; scans are made at document resolution, not photo resolution; and the occasional bulk offender (the 40MB video someone attached to a delivery note) is found by asking the host for the largest-files read when a jump appears.<br>• <b>The forecast conversation:</b> the trend line answers \"when do we need more\" months ahead, which converts a storage crisis into a plan-and-budget line with the host.</p><p><b>Performance.</b> Speed has no single gauge; the read is assembled:</p><p>1. <b>The user-report channel, disciplined:</b> \"slow\" reports are logged with the what/when/where (which screen, which report, what time, how many users) — the pattern across reports is the instrument. One person's slow afternoon is noise; everyone's slow month-end mornings is a signal with a shape.<br>2. <b>The reference timings:</b> a small set of standing operations timed monthly — the Trial Balance for the current month, one heavy standard report, one list view of the busiest doctype — the same operations, the same filters, logged on the health page. Drift across months is the evidence that survives the \"it feels slower\" argument in both directions.<br>3. <b>The background-jobs glance</b> (Chapter 4) riding the same monthly sitting.</p><p><b>The escalation, evidenced.</b> Performance raises to the host carry: the reference-timing trend, the pattern from the user reports (times, screens, concurrency), and the live-state evidence where captured. That package lets the host act in one pass — resource sizing, query attention, whatever the platform side needs — instead of a week of \"can you describe the slowness\".</p><p>Practical rule: both numbers are boring by design — two lines on the health page, five minutes monthly. The boredom is the point: the month either line becomes interesting, it does so with three months of context already attached.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The health page and the assembled reads", "12", "<p>The module's instruments assemble into one page and three cadences. This chapter is the assembly — the reads module 1's calendar promised, now fully built.</p><p><b>The health page.</b> One page, kept with the ops log, holding the standing numbers month by month:</p><p>• Storage: database size, files size, deltas (Chapter 6).<br>• The reference timings (Chapter 6).<br>• Error Log: new types this month, and the noise register (the known-benign list that keeps the daily read fast).<br>• Scheduler: failures this month, and any silence incidents.<br>• Email: queue error count, and the big runs confirmed (the statement spike).<br>• Users: enabled count, System Manager count (always two), lockouts, ninety-day-quiet count.<br>• Backups: presence-check misses (target zero), the drill's minutes (module 4's RTO evidence).<br>• Incidents: count by custody, with repeat-cause flags (Chapter 8).</p><p>Twelve columns a year. The page's value is the <b>row beside last month's row</b> — every trend rule in this module lands here.</p><p><b>The daily read (five minutes), assembled:</b></p><p>1. Error Log — last day, new types (Chapter 2).<br>2. Scheduled Job Log — the pulse and the failures (Chapter 3).<br>3. Email Queue — errors and the drain (module 6).<br>4. Backup presence (module 4).</p><p><b>The weekly read (ten minutes):</b> the people-read — failed-login clusters, lockouts, the user delta (Chapter 5) — plus the Background Jobs familiarity glance (Chapter 4).</p><p><b>The monthly read (thirty minutes):</b> the health page's row filled — storage, timings, the counts — the restore drill (module 4), the integration health checks (module 6), and the incident log's month read (Chapter 8). The row's anomalies each get one line of cause or one open action.</p><p><b>Delegation and continuity.</b> The reads are procedures with owners, which means they delegate: the daily read is an ideal early responsibility for the sysadmin's backstop (module 1's design) — it builds the recognise-normal skill that every other duty needs, and it means the reads survive leave and departure. The health page itself is the handover test's monitoring chapter: a newcomer reading twelve months of rows knows the system's normal better than most incumbents who never wrote it down.</p><p>Practical rule: resist adding numbers to the page. Every number must have a reader and a rule (what makes it a finding); a page of forty unowned metrics is a dashboard nobody reads, and the five-minute daily read stays five minutes only because the page stayed one page.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — The incident log & the HealthTrade case", "13", "<p><b>The incident log.</b> Every finding that required action — from any module — closes with a log entry: what, when detected, how detected (which read — the instrument's credit matters, Chapter 9 explains why), custody, cause, fix, duration, and the follow-up if any. The log's two standing reads:</p><p>• <b>Monthly:</b> the month's incidents counted by custody for the health page.<br>• <b>Quarterly (module 1's calendar):</b> the read <b>by cause</b> — the same cause twice is a process fault, and the fix moves upstream: the procedure amended, the checklist line added, the setting corrected. The quarterly read is where incidents stop repeating, which is the log's entire purpose.</p><p><b>The HealthTrade case — the monitoring year.</b></p><p><b>The reads installed.</b> The daily five-minute read starts in module 1's month three; this module completes the set: the health page opens with its first row (storage recorded from the host's numbers, the three reference timings logged — Trial Balance 4 seconds, the stock report 11, the invoice list 2), the noise register is built in the first fortnight (six known-benign error types listed, after which the daily Error Log scan drops to its promised sixty seconds), and the weekly and monthly sittings take their calendar slots.</p><p><b>The scheduler death, caught same-day (Chapter 3).</b> A Thursday daily read finds the Scheduled Job Log's newest entry aged nine hours — the pulse check's all-jobs-silent picture. Raised same-day at incident severity with the log timestamps; the host restores the scheduler by afternoon; the recovery checklist runs: the backup presence check confirms the night's set must be watched (the next nightly lands normally), two Auto Repeat entries that missed their slot are posted through the close's confirmed-fired line, and the queue drains its held mail. The incident log entry credits the read: <i>detected by daily pulse check, nine hours after failure; prior detection method: month-end, twenty days away.</i> The entry becomes the case for the calendar the next time anyone calls the daily read bureaucratic.</p><p><b>The slow month-end, escalated with evidence (Chapter 6).</b> Three users report slowness in the same close week; the discipline turns it into a package: the reports logged with times and screens (all morning, all report-heavy), the reference timings re-run mid-slowness (the stock report at 31 seconds against its 11-second baseline), the Background Jobs screenshot showing the aged queue. The raise lands with the host in one pass; the platform side resizes the site's resources; the next close's timings return to baseline — and the health page's timing row carries the whole story in three numbers: 11, 31, 12.</p><p><b>The storage jump, named in a month (Chapter 6).</b> Month five's files delta triples. The largest-files read from the host names the cause inside a day: a new branch process attaching full-resolution photos to every goods receipt. The fix is process, not storage: the scan-resolution guidance lands at the branch, the attachment size limit is confirmed doing its job, and the delta returns to trend. The quarterly incident read later flags nothing — one cause, one occurrence, closed.</p><p>The MD's line for the year file: <i>the system now tells us about its problems before we feel them — and the page proves it.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the seventh law", "11", "<p>The monitoring layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the unread instruments.</b> Symptom: the Error Log opened for the first time during an incident, holding six months of the answer; the scheduler dead for weeks; every failure felt before it is read. Cause: the instruments exist and the habit does not (Chapter 1). Fix: the three cadences installed with owners — the reads are minutes precisely because they are regular — and the incident log's how-detected field building the case that keeps them funded.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the census read.</b> Symptom: monitoring that takes an hour and gets skipped; dashboards of forty metrics nobody owns; the important exception buried in the unchanged. Cause: reads built as censuses instead of exceptions (Chapters 1, 7). Fix: exception-shaping — new types, deltas, silences — the noise register keeping the known out of the way, and the one-page rule on the health page.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the ignored silence.</b> Symptom: the missing backup nobody missed; the statement spike that did not happen; the job log quiet since Tuesday — all invisible because nothing looked wrong. Cause: reads that only scan for red (Chapter 1's third rule untaught). Fix: the expected-activity checks built into the reads — presence, pulse, spike — each one an absence made loud.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the evidence-free escalation.</b> Symptom: \"the system is slow\" raised with heat and nothing else; the host's week of diagnostic ping-pong; the slowness gone before it is measured. Cause: escalation without instruments (Chapters 4, 6). Fix: the discipline — live-state captures while the problem is live, reference timings for the drift argument, the five-item report armed with log entries — turning every raise into a one-pass action.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the repeating incident.</b> Symptom: the same failure quarterly, fixed the same way, surprising everyone each time. Cause: incidents closed without the log, or logged without the quarterly cause read (Chapter 8). Fix: the log as every finding's closing step, and the quarterly read moving fixes upstream — procedure, checklist, setting — until the cause stops producing.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the person-shaped monitor.</b> Symptom: monitoring that lives in one person's habits and dies with their leave; the backstop who has never run the daily read. Cause: reads as personal practice instead of owned procedure (Chapter 7). Fix: the reads written as procedures, the daily read delegated to the backstop as their standing apprenticeship, and the health page carrying the system's \"normal\" on paper where the handover test can find it.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the three reading rules and the two escalation lanes; triage an Error Log entry — new/known, custody, seen/silent — and name the four patterns; run the scheduler pulse check and its silence diagnosis, and the business-side verifications; read Background Jobs and translate the three user-facing situations; run the weekly people-read with its privacy posture; keep the storage and timing trends and build an evidenced escalation; assemble the health page and the three cadences with the one-page rule; and keep the incident log with its quarterly cause read.</p><p><b>The seventh law: the system is read before it is felt — numbers trended, exceptions investigated, and silence treated as a finding.</b> Module 8 closes the track: security, audit, and structure.</p>"),
]

